      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© Copyright 2016 by The American College of Veterinary Surgeons.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To describe the complications, short- and long-term outcomes, and owner satisfaction for dogs treated with pancarpal arthrodesis (PCA) using the 2.7/3.5 mm locking and dynamic compression plate (DCP) hybrid pancarpal arthrodesis plate (hybrid DCP [HDCP]) without rigid coaptation in the postoperative period."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
            "content": "Retrospective case series."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "ANIMALS",
            "content": "Dogs with carpal injuries requiring PCA (n=15)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Records (May 2007-October 2013) of dogs that had PCA using 3.5/2.7 mm HDCP (1 or 2 plates) and a Robert-Jones bandage were reviewed to determine intra- and post-operative complications and overall limb function. Follow-up was obtained by clinical evaluation at our institution and telephone interview of owners."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Fourteen dogs had PCA stabilized with a single HDCP and 1 giant breed dog had PCA stabilized with 2 HDCP on the dorsal aspect of the carpus secured onto the 3rd and 4th metacarpal bones. Two dogs presented with bilateral injuries. Thirteen owners (87%) judged the results of surgery as excellent and 2 (13%) judged the results of surgery as good. Minor complications (4 limbs; 23%) were occasional episodes of mild lameness in 3 dogs and 3 episodes of discharging sinus tracts in a single dog. Major complications (3 limbs; 17%) included 1 surgical site infection and implant failure in a 43 kg dog with an active leishmaniasis infection and a concurrent bilateral sacro-iliac luxation. Two dogs developed episodes of discharging sinus tract and pain over the most distal screw that eventually necessitated plate removal."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "PCA without rigid coaptation can successfully be performed using the HDCP."
          }
        ]
      },
